# Metaclaude configuration.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Then set, at minimum:
#   - CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN   (so the agent can actually run)
#   - METACLAUDE_BOOTSTRAP_USER / _PASSWORD  (to create the owner account)
#
# Everything else has a working default.
#
# One trap, if you edit this file by hand. Compose expands these values before
# the container sees them, so a dollar sign in a password or token is read as a
# variable: `pa$sword` arrives as `pa` and authentication fails with nothing in
# any log to explain it. Wrap such a value in double quotes and double the
# dollars — "pa$$sword" — which is also what deploy/bootstrap.sh writes for you.
# Quote it either way if it starts with ' or ", or compose rejects the file.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Claude credentials  ── required
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# To use your Pro or Max subscription (recommended — no per-token billing):
#
#   1. On a machine where you are signed in to Claude Code, run:
#        claude setup-token
#   2. Copy the token it prints and paste it below.
#
# The token is long-lived. Metaclaude passes it to the Claude CLI subprocess and
# never sends it anywhere else.
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=

# Alternative: pay-as-you-go API billing. Only used when the token above is
# empty. A subscription token always takes precedence.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# First-run account  ── required on first boot
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Used once, to create the owner account. After the first successful boot you
# can blank these out. The password must be at least 12 characters.
METACLAUDE_BOOTSTRAP_USER=owner
METACLAUDE_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# How it is reached, and how TLS is obtained
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# TLS is not optional. This app sets a Secure cookie and registers a service
# worker; both need a secure context, so over plain HTTP the browser refuses to
# store the session and the app does not work at all.
#
# METACLAUDE_SITE is the address you type into a browser — an IP, or a hostname.
METACLAUDE_SITE=localhost

# A second address the same application answers on, always under Caddy's own CA.
#
# This exists for the day a domain is pointed at a server that until then was
# reached by IP. Moving METACLAUDE_SITE to the name moves the certificate with
# it, and the address everyone had bookmarked starts failing the handshake.
# Naming it here keeps it working. deploy/bootstrap.sh fills this in for you.
#
# The default is a `.invalid` name, reserved by RFC 2606 and resolvable
# nowhere, so the block parses and nothing outside can reach it. It must not be
# `localhost`: that is METACLAUDE_SITE's own default, and two site blocks at the
# same address make Caddy refuse the entire file.
METACLAUDE_ALT_SITE=alt.metaclaude.invalid

# Which certificate to serve to a client that sent no SNI at all, and to one
# whose SNI matches no site.
#
# RFC 6066 forbids an IP literal in SNI, so every browser reaching this server
# by address rather than by name sends none — which makes this, in practice,
# "the server's own address". It is separate from METACLAUDE_SITE because once
# a domain is in use the two are different values.
#
# Whatever you put here must be an address one of the two site blocks actually
# serves — METACLAUDE_SITE or METACLAUDE_ALT_SITE. Caddy picks among the
# certificates it *has*; naming a third address leaves it nothing to fall back
# to, and the handshake then dies with `tlsv1 alert internal error` before a
# single log line is written. Leaving this commented out is safe: it then
# follows METACLAUDE_SITE, which by construction has a certificate.
METACLAUDE_SNI_DEFAULT=localhost

# Which docker/tls/<mode>.caddy is used. Each file documents its own trade-offs;
# docs/DEPLOYMENT.md compares them.
#
#   internal   Caddy's own CA. The only mode that needs nothing but an IP and no
#              third party. Install the root certificate once per device, or
#              every visit warns and iOS will not treat the origin as secure.
#   acme-dns   Public Let's Encrypt for a hostname that resolves here.
#   acme-dns-staging
#              The same, against Let's Encrypt's staging directory. Untrusted,
#              so browsers warn and the app does not work — but effectively
#              unlimited, which is what you want while proving the chain works.
#              Production allows five duplicate certificates per week for one
#              name, and a reinstall that discards the caddy-data volume spends
#              one every time. Rehearse here, then switch to acme-dns.
#   acme-ip    Public Let's Encrypt for the bare IP. Real certificate, nothing
#              to install — but 160-hour lifetimes and five issuances per week
#              maximum, so renewal has to genuinely work.
#   file       A certificate obtained elsewhere. The Tailscale route: a real
#              hostname and a real certificate without owning a domain, and no
#              inbound ports open at all.
METACLAUDE_TLS_MODE=internal

# Contact address for the ACME modes. Ignored by internal and file.
#
# Required by acme-dns and acme-ip, and the literal below is a trap on those:
# `internal` is substituted straight into Caddy's `tls` directive, where it is
# the keyword meaning "use Caddy's own CA". Leaving it here while switching
# METACLAUDE_TLS_MODE to acme-dns therefore produces a self-signed deployment
# with no error anywhere — a browser warning instead of the public certificate
# you asked for. deploy/bootstrap.sh refuses the hostname path without --email
# for this reason.
METACLAUDE_TLS_EMAIL=internal

# acme-ip only, and only while testing: production allows five certificates per
# identifier set per seven days with no override, so iterate against staging.
#   https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory
METACLAUDE_ACME_DIR=

# file mode only: reads /certs/<name>.crt and /certs/<name>.key.
METACLAUDE_CERT_NAME=metaclaude
METACLAUDE_CERTS_DIR=./docker/certs

# Which interface the proxy binds to. A security control, not a convenience:
# Docker's firewall rules are consulted before ufw's, so a port published on
# 0.0.0.0 is reachable from the internet whatever ufw says. Naming the interface
# here is what actually holds.
#
#   0.0.0.0      public — the server is reached at its own IP
#   100.x.y.z    the Tailscale/WireGuard address — nothing else is exposed
#   127.0.0.1    local only, for an SSH tunnel
#
# The default is the closed one deliberately: an unset or mistyped value should
# make the service unreachable, not world-readable.
METACLAUDE_BIND=127.0.0.1
METACLAUDE_HTTP_PORT=80
METACLAUDE_HTTPS_PORT=443

# Which HTTP versions the proxy offers. Set this to "h1 h2" when
# METACLAUDE_TLS_MODE=internal: Chrome refuses QUIC against a locally-installed
# trust anchor, so h3 is advertised, declined and falls back to TCP anyway.
METACLAUDE_PROTOCOLS=h1 h2 h3

# Extra origins allowed to call the API. Leave empty: an IP, an IP with a port,
# and a Tailscale name are all recognised as same-origin without it.
METACLAUDE_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# The image to run. Left empty, compose builds from this checkout, which is what
# you want locally. A deployment writes the digest it resolved into
# releases/.env.image, so nothing here needs editing on the server.
METACLAUDE_IMAGE=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Encryption
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# 32 bytes, hex-encoded, used to encrypt stored MCP secrets.
# Generate one with:  openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Set this explicitly on a real deployment. Left empty, one is generated into
# /var/lib/metaclaude/master.key — inside the same volume as the database it
# encrypts, so a single snapshot carries both the ciphertext and the key that
# opens it, and the encryption buys nothing against a stolen backup.
#
# Worse, restoring a database without its key silently mints a new one: the
# server starts, reports healthy, and every stored MCP credential is quietly
# gone. Keep the hex string in your password manager.
METACLAUDE_MASTER_KEY=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Agent behaviour
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# How many agent runs may execute at once. Each is a Claude CLI subprocess.
METACLAUDE_MAX_CONCURRENT_RUNS=4

# The board autopilot waits above this percentage of the plan's worst quota
# window before starting a card by itself (100 disables the guard). It only
# gates automatic starts — a human pressing the button is never refused.
METACLAUDE_QUOTA_GUARD_PCT=85

# Hard ceiling on a single run's wall-clock time (ms). Default 45 minutes.
METACLAUDE_RUN_TIMEOUT_MS=2700000

# How long a finished run and its transcript are kept, in days. 0 keeps
# everything forever.
#
# `transcript_events` holds every message, tool call and streamed delta of
# every run, and it is the one table with no natural ceiling — the audit log
# and the distilled insights both already had a one-year window, this one had
# none. Six months is deliberately generous: this is the only background sweep
# that destroys something you wrote.
METACLAUDE_RUN_RETENTION_DAYS=180

# Newest runs kept per workspace whatever their age.
#
# Age alone would empty a workspace nobody has opened in a year, and losing the
# only three runs someone has is a far worse outcome than a few megabytes. A
# run is deleted only when it is past *both* conditions.
METACLAUDE_RUN_KEEP_PER_WORKSPACE=50

# Unlock the `bypassPermissions` mode, in which the agent runs commands and
# edits files without ever asking. Leave this empty unless the container is
# genuinely disposable — it removes the last safety check.
METACLAUDE_ALLOW_BYPASS_PERMISSIONS=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Learning
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   hash  — the built-in embedder. No download, no network, works immediately.
#   local — a sentence-transformer running on-device. Better semantic recall,
#           at the cost of a ~90 MB model download on first use.
#
# `local` needs `@huggingface/transformers`, which is deliberately not a
# dependency of any package and is not in the shipped image: the download and
# the ~90 MB are an opt-in, not something every deployment pays for. Without it
# the provider falls back to `hash` and logs that it did — so setting `local`
# here on a stock image changes nothing except that line in the log.
#
# Switching between them changes the vector space, so run
# Memory → Maintenance → Re-index afterwards.
# hash  — built-in, no download, no network. Matches words well and meaning
#         not at all: a question sharing no vocabulary with its answer finds
#         nothing (measured). This is the default and it works everywhere.
# local — a sentence-transformer, which crosses that wall. REQUIRES adding
#         @huggingface/transformers to the image (~350 MB with onnxruntime)
#         and outbound access to huggingface.co for the first download.
#         Without both, the app falls back to `hash` and the doctor warns.
#         After switching, re-index from Memory → Knowledge and Memory →
# The VAPID `sub` claim for web push — who a push relay would contact about a
# misbehaving sender. Relays deliver nothing to it, but they do validate it:
# Apple answers 400 BadJwtToken for a token it dislikes, and a `mailto:` on the
# reserved `.invalid` TLD is exactly the kind it dislikes. A mailto: or https:
# URL, nothing else.
# METACLAUDE_PUSH_SUBJECT=https://github.com/metaclaude

# The address this deployment answers on, scheme included — for example
# https://metaclaude.example.com. Needed by exactly one thing: authorising an
# MCP server over OAuth, where the authorization server has to be able to send
# your browser back to /api/mcp/oauth/callback here.
#
# It cannot be derived from the request. A Host header is set by whoever is
# calling, and a redirect URI is the one value in OAuth that must never come
# from the client — so it is stated once, by you, or the flow refuses and says
# which setting is missing. Everything else runs untouched without it.
# METACLAUDE_PUBLIC_URL=https://metaclaude.example.com

#         Maintenance, or old vectors stay incomparable.
METACLAUDE_EMBEDDINGS=hash
METACLAUDE_EMBEDDING_MODEL=Xenova/all-MiniLM-L6-v2

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Resources and misc
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Left empty on purpose: compose then applies its own defaults, which are sized
# to start on the smallest VPS anyone would use. Raise them to suit your server —
# deploy/bootstrap.sh does it for you, from the host's real core count and RAM.
#
# Do not set the CPU limit above the number of cores the machine actually has.
# Docker does not clamp it, it refuses to create the container outright:
# "range of CPUs is from 0.01 to 2.00, as there are only 2 CPUs available".
# The stack then never starts, and the error appears at `up`, not before.
METACLAUDE_CPU_LIMIT=
METACLAUDE_MEMORY_LIMIT=

# The proxy's own limits. Much smaller, and deliberately: a TLS terminator for
# one site needs very little, and the point of bounding it is that it cannot
# compete with the agent for the host's memory. Raise only if you are serving
# something unusual through it.
METACLAUDE_PROXY_CPU_LIMIT=1
METACLAUDE_PROXY_MEMORY_LIMIT=256m

# Identity the agent commits under.
GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=Metaclaude
GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=metaclaude@localhost

# Where Settings → System → Updates looks for published releases (owner/repo
# on GitHub). Informational only — applying an update stays the tag-driven,
# health-gated deploy pipeline. Set empty to disable the check entirely.
METACLAUDE_UPDATE_REPO=jgouviergmail/Metaclaude

TZ=UTC
LOG_LEVEL=info
